        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Vinča culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, mainly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> but also parts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>), western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>; key sites Vinča-Belo Brdo near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, Pločnik, Rudna Glava, Divostin, Crkvine-Stubline, Gomolava, Selevac, Tărtăria and Vršac</span></div>
