    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Vinča settlements were larger than those of almost every other European culture of their time; Vinča-Belo Brdo covered 29 hectares with up to 2,500 people.</li><li>The Vinča site of Pločnik has produced the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> tools in the world, though copper was used mostly for ornaments while working tools stayed of stone, bone and antler.</li><li>Whether the culture grew out of the local Starčevo culture or came with a new movement of people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> is still undecided.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Vinča symbols incised on objects of the culture are conjectured by some to be the earliest form of proto-writing.</p></aside>
    </section>

