    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">5400/5300 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Vinča culture began, according to 600 radiocarbon dates published in 2016</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">4900–4650 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Divostin site was occupied twice, with an estimated 1,028 houses built</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">4850/4800 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Crkvine-Stubline was founded, perhaps housing up to 4,000 people</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 4850–4500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In the Vinča D phase the centre of the network shifted from Vinča-Belo Brdo to Vršac</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 4500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Vinča network lost its cohesion and fell into decline</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1908</span><span class="rf-text">Miloje Vasić discovered the type site, Vinča-Belo Brdo</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Vinča culture, also called the Turdaș or Vinča-Turdaș culture, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> culture of Southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> dated to 5400–4500 BCE. It is named for its type site, Vinča-Belo Brdo, a large tell settlement near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> discovered by the Serbian archaeologist Miloje Vasić in 1908. Radiocarbon dating has narrowed its span: 155 dates published in 2009 placed the type site at 5400/5300–4650/4600 BCE, and 600 dates in 2016 placed the culture between 5400/5300 and 4500 BCE.</p><p>Its origins are still debated, and Marko Porčić wrote in 2016 that there is not enough evidence to accept or reject any of the hypotheses. One, proposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colin-renfrew">Colin Renfrew</a> and Ruth Tringham, has it develop locally from the Starčevo culture; but the earliest southern sites seem as early as the northern ones and lack local continuity. The other, proposed by V. Gordon Childe and Milutin Garašanin, sees a second movement of people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. Archaeogenetic studies of 15 individuals found mostly Y-DNA haplogroup G2a and about 90–97% Early European Farmer ancestry, closest to Neolithic Anatolia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Farming brought to the region by the First Temperate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> was developed further under the Vinča culture, fuelling a population boom. Settlements fell into sizes of about 1–1.9, 4–4.9 and 20–29 hectares, and some outgrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and Near Eastern cities of a millennium later. Vinča-Belo Brdo covered 29 hectares with up to 2,500 people, Crkvine-Stubline may have held 4,000, and Parța perhaps 1,575. Early densities were 50–200 people per hectare. Yet there is no evidence of proto-urbanism or of specialised military, religious or administrative centres, though settlements had defences, and the network was probably not politically unified.</p><p>Most people worked at producing food in a mixed economy of farming, herding, hunting and foraging. Vinča farmers introduced common wheat, oats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> to temperate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and made more use of barley, and there is indirect evidence for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-drawn plough. Cattle mattered more than sheep and goats and were kept for milk, leather and draught. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herders</a> took livestock to the uplands each season. Deer, boar and aurochs were hunted and carp and catfish caught. Crafts were made in the household, with little sign of specialists.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like other cultures of its age, the Vinča culture is distinguished chiefly by its settlement pattern and its ritual behaviour. Figurines in various zoomorphic and anthropomorphic styles were among its hallmarks, and the cohesion of its scattered settlements was kept up by the long-distance exchange of ritual items, the figurines among them. The Vinča C phase, also called the “Vinča C shock” or Gradac phase, saw an increase in ritual figurines alongside changes in pottery, settlement and metalworking; the change was strongest in the South-Moravian and Kosovian variants of the culture.</p><p>The Vinča symbols, marks incised on objects, are among the culture’s most discussed features. Some conjecture that they are the earliest form of proto-writing, a reading that remains a matter of debate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Marija Gimbutas</a> placed the culture within her “Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>”, which she described as relatively homogeneous, peaceful and matrifocal. That view was widely held until the 1990s, when evidence of violent massacres and defensively enclosed settlements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> began to emerge.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The culture was particularly noted for its distinctive dark-burnished pottery. Some Vinča wares were made with considerable technical skill: a two-stage method produced pottery with a polished, multicoloured finish known as Black-topped and Rainbow Ware, and powdered cinnabar and limonite were sometimes applied to the fired clay as decoration. The figurines were made in many styles, animal and human.</p><p>Figurines also show how people dressed, in open-necked tunics and decorated skirts. Cloth was woven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> and wool, flax becoming more important later, and buttons were made of shell or stone. In metalworking the Vinča culture featured the earliest known example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">copper smelting</a>, though it is not usually counted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> ores were mined on a large scale at Rudna Glava, but only a fraction was smelted and cast, into ornaments and trinkets rather than tools, which stayed of chipped stone, bone and antler. Most mined ore was probably used as powder, for pottery or for decorating the body. In the late Vinča D phase the first toggling harpoon appeared.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Vinča region had already been settled by farming societies of the First Temperate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, such as the Starčevo culture, and the culture showed strong links with its neighbours: Karanovo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, Precucuteni-Tripolye A in Moldavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, Dimini in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and the late Starčevo and early Sopot cultures in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. Its settlements, spread across the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, kept a high degree of cultural uniformity through the long-distance exchange of ritual items, though they were probably not politically unified.</p><p>Exchange shifted in the late Vinča D phase. The centre of the network moved from Vinča-Belo Brdo to Vršac, and the long-distance trade in obsidian from modern-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Spondylus shell from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> became more important than the exchange of figurines. Settlements, though they show no specialised military centres, had defensive works. Seasonal movement into the uplands was driven partly by the search for stone and minerals, and where these were rich, permanent upland settlements were founded that relied more on herding.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Eventually the Vinča network lost its cohesion and fell into decline. After two millennia of intensive farming, economic stress from falling soil fertility was probably partly responsible. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Gimbutas</a> argued that the decline was followed by an invasion of horse-riding Proto-Indo-European tribes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a>, a view since criticised as “New Age sentiment” and prevalent until the 1990s, when evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> massacres and fortified settlements emerged; the debate over the culture’s end, like its origins, remains open.</p><p>The culture is remembered for some of the largest settlements of prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, surpassed in its time only by the Cucuteni–Trypillia culture, and for the beginnings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> metallurgy. Its genetic profile links it to the Neolithic movement of farmers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> into central Europe, and a 2021 study found that Neolithic farmers, the Vinča among them, produced lower levels of inflammatory cytokines than earlier hunter-gatherers, a trait that has passed into the immune system of modern Europeans. Its type site, Vinča-Belo Brdo, is today a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Vinča culture flourished in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> from about 5400 to 4500 BCE, its farmers building some of the largest settlements of prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Known for dark-burnished pottery, figurines, the debated Vinča symbols and the earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">copper smelting</a>, its network declined after two millennia of intensive farming.</em></p>

